Proof. First consider the case where every atom of L is also a coatom. This forces K = L - \{\hat{0},\hat{1}\}. And the meet and join conditions are satisfied for all B \subseteq K with \#B \geq 2. If \#K = n, then, using Theorem 1.3.3(d),    \[    \sum_{\substack{B \subseteq K \  igvee B = \hat{1}}} (-1)^{\#B} = \sum_{k=2}^{n}  inom{n}{k} (-1)^k = -\sum_{k=0}^{1}  inom{n}{k} (-1)^k = n - 1 = \mu(\hat{1}).    \]    Now assume that the atom and coatom sets of L do not coincide. By Exercise 12(c) and 23(c), the theorem holds for L if and only if it holds for L^*. So, by taking the dual if necessary, we can assume that there is a coatom c 
otin K. We now induct on \#L. The previous paragraph takes care of the case \#L = 3 and smaller lattices do not have crosscuts. Suppose that \#L \geq 4 and let \sim be the equivalence relation of Lemma 5.8.1. Since c, \hat{1} 
otin K, the [x] for x \in K form a crosscut for P/\sim which we will denote by [K] and its subsets by [B]. By induction,    \[    \mu([\hat{1}]) = \sum_{\substack{[B] \subseteq [K] \  igvee [B] = [\hat{1}] \  igwedge [B] = [\hat{0}]}} (-1)^{\#[B]}.    \]    By Lemma 5.8.1 we have  igvee [B] = [\hat{1}] if and only if  igvee B = c or \hat{1}. By the same token and the fact that c, \hat{1} 
otin K we get that  igwedge [B] = [\hat{0}] is equivalent to  igwedge B = \hat{0}. So the previous displayed equation becomes    \[    \mu([\hat{1}]) = \sum_{\substack{B \subseteq K \  igvee B = c \  igwedge B = \hat{0}}} (-1)^{\#B} + \sum_{\substack{B \subseteq K \  igvee B = \hat{1} \  igwedge B = \hat{0}}} (-1)^{\#B}.    \]    Note that since K is a crosscut of L not containing c we have that K' := K \cap [\hat{0}, c] is a crosscut of [\hat{0}, c]. Furthermore,  igvee B = c implies that B \subseteq K'. So applying induction to the sum with this restriction gives    \[    \mu([\hat{1}]) = \mu(c) + \sum_{\substack{B \subseteq K \  igvee B = \hat{1} \  igwedge B = \hat{0}}} (-1)^{\#B}.    \]    A rearrangement of terms and Lemma 5.8.1 completes the demonstration.

